rsetup-board-agent — notes for Debian users
===========================================

What this package provides
--------------------------

  /usr/bin/rsetup-board-agent                board-side agent binary
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/rsetup-board-agent.service
  /var/lib/rsetup-board-agent                durable task journal (state, mode 0700)

The unit is installed but neither enabled nor started
(dh_installsystemd --no-enable), and because of that the package carries no
systemd maintainer snippet either — installing or upgrading it does not run
`systemctl daemon-reload` for you. To run the read-only sampler on this board:

  sudo systemctl daemon-reload
  sudo systemctl enable --now rsetup-board-agent
  journalctl -u rsetup-board-agent -f

Read this before you enable it
------------------------------

This package delivers the agent's read-only half, and nothing more:

  * The production encrypted tunnel is NOT wired up. The unit starts the binary's
    --watch loop, i.e. a local read-only sampling of device.status.get every 10
    seconds written to journald. The process opens no session with a controller —
    the wire protocol codec has not been approved, so there is nothing to connect
    to yet.
  * A real host reboot boundary is NOT implemented. Both device.reboot.prepare
    and device.reboot.execute answer "capability unavailable"; the binary and the
    unit never reboot the machine.

Enabling the unit therefore gives you an always-on local probe, not a controllable
board. Do not record the control path (in particular reboot) as closed-loop on the
strength of this package.

Running user: root
------------------

The unit runs as User=root/Group=root, without a dedicated unprivileged account
and without DynamicUser. The agent reads the whole board's hardware and system
state (/proc and /sys temperatures, load, memory, the kernel boot_id, and the
firmware/partition/power sources that will be added later); those sources are not
uniformly readable by an unprivileged user, and running as one root process under
the sandbox below is preferable to widening a sudo/polkit allow-list per reading.

Because the process is root, the unit keeps the surrounding restrictions:
ProtectSystem=strict makes /usr and /etc read-only for it, ProtectHome=yes hides
home directories, PrivateTmp=yes, NoNewPrivileges=yes and LockPersonality=yes are
set, and /var/lib/rsetup-board-agent (created 0700 by StateDirectory) is the only
path it may write to. RestrictAddressFamilies permits IPv4/IPv6/Unix sockets only —
the current --watch mode binds no socket at all; the allowance is there so the unit
needs no change once the tunnel is actually connected.

Local, non-systemd runs
-----------------------

Run by hand, the binary runs as whoever invokes it (no privilege elevation of any
kind), for example:

  rsetup-board-agent --once
  rsetup-board-agent --watch --interval-secs 10
  rsetup-board-agent --loopback-test-server --listen 127.0.0.1:39876

The last mode is a test-only JSON-lines console on loopback; it is not the
production wire protocol and must not be exposed. Some readings require root to be
meaningful, which is why the packaged unit runs as root while a manual run stays
with the invoking user.
